WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.280
Very, very deep neural networks are difficult to train because of vanishing and exploding

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:07.400
gradient types of problems.

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:12.080
In this video, you learn about skip connections, which allows you to take the activation from

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:17.560
one layer and suddenly feed it to another layer even much deeper in the neural network.

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:22.000
And using that, you really build resnets, which enables you to train very, very deep

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:25.840
networks, sometimes even networks of over 100 layers.

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:27.400
Let's take a look.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:33.360
And so built out of something called a residual block, let's first describe what that is.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:37.560
Here are two layers of a neural network where you start off with some activation in layer

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:44.120
L, then you go to AL plus one, and then the activation two layers later is AL plus two.

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:49.400
So to go through the steps in this computation, you have AL.

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:55.880
And then the first thing you do is you apply this linear operator to it, which is governed

00:00:55.880 --> 00:01:04.680
by this equation to go from AL to compute ZL plus one by multiplying by the weight matrix

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:08.240
and adding that bias vector.

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:18.160
After that, you apply the ReLU linearity to get AL plus one.

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:25.040
And that's governed by this equation where AL plus one is G of ZL plus one.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:30.760
And then the next layer, you apply this linear step again.

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:33.560
So it's governed by that equation, right?

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:38.120
So this is quite similar to this equation we saw on the left.

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:47.200
And then finally, you apply another ReLU operation, which is now governed by that equation,

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:52.560
where G here would be the ReLU nonlinearity.

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:57.040
And this gives you AL plus two.

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:05.400
So in other words, for information from AL to flow to AL plus two, it needs to go through

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:13.440
all of these steps, which I'm going to call the main path of this set of layers.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:16.880
In a residual net, we're going to make a change to this.

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:25.320
So we're going to take AL and just fast forward it, copy it much further into the neural network

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:34.520
to here, and just add AL before applying to nonlinearity, the ReLU nonlinearity.

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:37.840
And I'm going to call this the shortcut.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:43.200
So rather than needing to follow the main path, the information from AL can now follow

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:47.120
a shortcut to go much deeper into the neural network.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:52.800
And what that means is that this last equation goes away, and we instead have that the output

00:02:52.800 --> 00:03:02.960
AL plus two is the ReLU nonlinearity G applied to ZL plus two as before, but now plus AL.

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:08.520
So the addition of this AL here, it mixes a residual block.

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:14.760
And in pictures, you can also modify this picture on top by drawing this extra shortcut

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:15.760
to go here.

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:22.720
And we're going to draw it as going into this second layer here, because the shortcut is

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:26.080
actually added before the ReLU nonlinearity.

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:30.680
So each of these nodes here, where that applies a linear function and a ReLU.

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:35.560
So AL is being injected after the linear part, but before the ReLU part.

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:40.680
And sometimes instead of the term shortcut, you also hear the term skip connection.

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:45.480
And that refers to AL just skipping over a layer or kind of skipping over almost two

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:51.080
layers in order to pass this information deeper into the neural network.

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:57.040
So what the inventors of ResNet, so that would be Kaiming He, Xiang Yu Zhang, Xiaoxing

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:03.640
Ren, and Jian Sun, what they found was that using residual blocks allows you to train

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:06.160
much deeper neural networks.

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:11.320
And the way you build a ResNet is by taking many of these residual blocks, blocks like

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:16.040
these, and stacking them together to form a deep network.

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:18.240
So let's look at this network.

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:20.640
This is not a residual network.

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:22.640
This called as a plain network.

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:27.200
This is the terminology of the ResNet paper.

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:32.760
To turn this into a ResNet, what you do is you add all those skip connections, although

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:36.720
short circuit connections like so.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:45.400
So every two layers ends up with that additional change that we saw on the previous

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:49.560
slide to turn each of these into a residual block.

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:55.680
So this picture shows five residual blocks stacked together, and this is a residual

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:56.560
network.

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:02.240
And it turns out that if you use, you know, a standard optimization algorithm,

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:06.400
such as Brain InterSense, or one of the fancy optimization algorithms, to train a

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:12.520
plain network, so without all the extra residual, without all the extra shortcuts

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:14.640
or skip connections I just drew in.

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:19.520
Empirically, you find that as you increase the number of layers, the training

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:24.240
error will tend to decrease after a while, but then it'll tend to go back up.

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:30.520
And in theory, as you make a neural network deeper, you know, it should only

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:33.160
do better and better on the training set.

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:38.000
So in theory, having a deeper network should only help.

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:43.480
But in practice or in reality, having a plain network, so not a ResNet, but having a plain

00:05:43.480 --> 00:05:48.600
network that's very deep means that your optimization algorithm just has a much

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:50.120
harder time training.

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:55.240
And so in reality, your training error gets worse if you pick a network that's too

00:05:55.240 --> 00:05:55.840
deep.

00:05:55.840 --> 00:06:01.760
But what happens with ResNet is that even as the number of layers gets deeper, you

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:06.360
can have the performance of the training error kind of keep on going down.

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:10.640
You know, even if we train a network with over 100 layers, and then now some

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:15.200
people experimenting with networks, there are over a thousand layers, although I

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:18.080
don't see that used much in practice yet.

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:22.960
But by taking these activations, be it X or these intermediate activations, and

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:27.840
allowing it to go much deeper in the neural network, this really helps with the

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:32.480
vanishing and exploding gradient problems and allows you to train much deeper

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:36.160
neural networks without really appreciable loss in performance.

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:39.560
And maybe at some point, this will plateau, this will flatten out, and it

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:43.040
doesn't help that much the deeper and deeper networks.

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:49.200
But ResNet has certainly been effective at helping train very deep networks.

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:52.600
So you've now gotten an overview of how ResNet work.

00:06:52.600 --> 00:06:57.120
And in fact, in this week's program exercise, you get to implement these ideas

00:06:57.120 --> 00:06:59.440
and see it work for yourself.

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:03.520
But next, I want to share with you better intuition or even more intuition

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:06.440
about why ResNet works so well.

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:07.600
Let's go on to the next video.
